const ap = (x, o) => para(x, { spacing: { after: 130, line: 300 }, ...(o || {}) });
const abstract = [
  ...unnumbered('Abstract'),
  ap('Faulty streetlights reported by citizens are still handled manually in most Indian cities. A clerk reads each phone call, WhatsApp message, email or tweet, decides how urgent it is, tries to work out which pole is meant, and calls a technician. The result is delayed repairs, inefficient crew scheduling and unsafe dark roads. This report presents **Prakash**, an AI-driven streetlight maintenance and decision support system that automates this journey end to end.'),
  ap('Prakash accepts complaints from six channels (WhatsApp, SMS, email, X/Twitter, call-centre transcripts and a web portal) and processes every message with one Natural Language Processing pipeline: channel-specific text clean-up, language identification for English, Hindi, Marathi and Hinglish, fault classification into eight classes using TF-IDF word and character n-grams with logistic regression, named entity recognition for pole IDs, landmarks, roads, sectors, durations and fault phrases, and an explainable severity score from 0 to 100. The extracted location is geocoded, snapped to the nearest lamp in a GIS lamp register and checked against open tickets so that duplicate reports merge instead of creating extra work. Messages the system is unsure about are sent to a human review queue, and the operator\'s corrections are used to retrain the classifier.'),
  ap('On top of the NLP layer, Prakash identifies maintenance hotspots with DBSCAN clustering, predicts the probability that each lamp fails within 14 days with a gradient-boosted model, forecasts weekly fault counts with damped Holt smoothing, and builds crew routes with a priority-, skill- and shift-aware insertion heuristic improved by 2-opt and or-opt moves. A role-based web application (citizen, technician, admin) with live updates over server-sent events, alerts, a technician route view and a citizen tracking portal puts the results into use. The backend is written in Python with Flask and SQLite; the interface uses Leaflet and Chart.js.'),
  ap('On a held-out test of 480 paraphrased complaints that never appear in training, the classifier reaches **87.5% accuracy (macro-F1 0.875)** against 42.7% for the keyword-rule baseline of our first prototype, and 91.7% on 24 hand-written messages. The failure-risk model reaches an AUC of 0.80, and on the demonstration data the route optimiser cuts total travel by about one third and reduces the average time to reach Critical and High jobs from 105 to 12 minutes. Because no public labelled streetlight-complaint corpus exists, the training complaints, lamp network and history are synthetic; the report states this limitation openly and describes how the system retrains on real data. The project supports **SDG 11**, in particular target 11.7 (safe public spaces), target 11.2 (safe transport systems) and target 11.3 (participatory urban planning).'),
  ap('**Keywords:** Natural Language Processing, text classification, named entity recognition, code-mixed text, GIS, DBSCAN, predictive maintenance, route optimisation, human-in-the-loop, SDG 11.', { run: {} }),
];

