const ch2 = [
  ...chapter(2, 'Literature Review and Technical Background'),
  sec('2.1 Citizen Complaint Systems'),
  para('Several civic reporting platforms already let residents report faulty infrastructure. FixMyStreet (mySociety) and SeeClickFix are widely used examples, and many municipalities run their own 311-style helplines and mobile apps. Their strength is easy intake and public visibility of reports. Most depend on structured form fields (category, pin on a map) filled in by the citizen, or on staff who read and categorise free text. Open 311 data sets such as the New York City 311 Service Requests [18] and the City of Chicago 311 Service Requests [19] contain street-light complaints and show how large the volumes are, but they are English-only and structured. Free-text, multilingual and code-mixed reports through chat channels are much less served, and that is the gap this project targets.'),
  sec('2.2 Text Classification'),
  para('Classifying short complaints into a small set of fault types is a supervised text-classification task. A standard and strong baseline represents each message as a vector of **TF-IDF** weights [2] and trains a linear classifier such as logistic regression [20]. Term frequency rewards words that appear often in a message, and inverse document frequency down-weights words that appear in almost every message. Character n-gram features extend the idea to sub-word units, which makes the representation robust to spelling variation and to transliteration, both common in Indian-language chat messages.'),
  para('Pre-trained transformer models such as BERT [10] and its smaller variant DistilBERT [11] give higher accuracy when enough labelled data and compute are available, and multilingual models such as MuRIL [12] are trained specifically on Indian languages and their transliterated forms. We chose the TF-IDF and logistic-regression route for this project because there is no labelled real data, training must finish in seconds on a laptop, and a linear model is transparent: the weight of each word in each decision can be shown to the operator. The pipeline is modular so a transformer can replace the classifier later.'),
  sec('2.3 Named Entity Recognition and Geoparsing'),
  para('Named Entity Recognition (NER) finds and labels spans of text that refer to entities such as places or durations [4]. Approaches range from hand-written rules and gazetteers (lists of known names) to statistical sequence models and neural taggers. For complaints, the useful entities are domain-specific: pole identifiers, landmarks, roads, sectors, durations and fault phrases. With no annotated data, a hybrid of regular expressions and a gazetteer of local landmarks is the practical choice, and it is easy to extend. Geoparsing, the step of turning a recognised place name into coordinates, is done by gazetteer look-up; coordinates are then matched to the nearest asset using great-circle distance computed with the haversine formula [22].'),
  sec('2.4 Code-Mixed and Multilingual Text'),
  para('Messages that mix Hindi and English, either in Devanagari or in Roman script ("Hinglish"), are routine in India. They break word-level tokenisers trained on a single language and produce many unseen spellings. Character n-grams, script detection, and training data that deliberately includes the mixed forms are lightweight ways to handle them. The system detects the language so that its automatic reply can be written in the citizen\'s own language.'),
  sec('2.5 Spatial Clustering for Hotspots'),
  para('DBSCAN (Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise) [5] groups points that have at least a minimum number of neighbours within a given radius, and labels isolated points as noise. It needs no preset number of clusters and can find clusters of any shape, which suits faults that line up along a road because they share a feeder or a cable. Using the haversine metric makes the radius a real distance in metres.'),
  sec('2.6 Predictive Maintenance and Forecasting'),
  para('Predictive maintenance estimates the probability that an asset fails within a horizon, using features such as age, past failures and operating conditions. Gradient-boosted decision trees [6] are a strong, widely used model for such tabular data and provide feature importance for explanation. For counts over time, exponential smoothing with a damped trend [7] is a simple, robust forecaster that works with short histories, and it supports prediction intervals from the spread of past errors.'),
  sec('2.7 Vehicle Routing and Scheduling'),
  para('Assigning jobs to crews and ordering the visits is a Vehicle Routing Problem (VRP) with additional constraints [9]. Exact methods do not scale well, so practical systems use heuristics. Cheapest insertion builds routes by adding each job at the position that increases cost least, and local-search moves such as 2-opt [8] (reverse a segment of the route) and or-opt (move a job) improve them. Skill constraints (only certified crews handle live-wire hazards) and shift length are modelled as hard limits.'),
  sec('2.8 Research Gap and Contribution'),
  table([3000, 6026], ['Gap in existing practice', 'How Prakash addresses it'], [
    ['Reports need structured forms or manual reading', 'One NLP pipeline reads free text from six channels.'],
    ['English-only processing', 'English, Hindi, Marathi and Hinglish handled by the same model and answered in the citizen\'s language.'],
    ['Location lost in words', 'NER plus gazetteer, pole-ID look-up and GPS pins resolve a message to a specific lamp.'],
    ['Duplicates and flat queues', 'Reports about one lamp merge and raise its priority; severity is explainable.'],
    ['Reactive maintenance only', 'Failure-risk prediction and hotspot detection support preventive work orders.'],
    ['Crews dispatched by arrival order', 'Skill-, priority- and distance-aware optimiser compared with a manual baseline.'],
    ['AI decisions are opaque', 'Every decision shows the words, rules and points behind it; low confidence goes to a human, and corrections retrain the model.'],
  ], 'Gaps addressed by this work'),
];

