const ch6 = [
  ...chapter(6, 'Predictive Analytics and Optimisation'),
  sec('6.1 Hotspot Detection with DBSCAN'),
  para('Two kinds of hotspot are computed. **Current hotspots** are found by running DBSCAN with the haversine metric on the coordinates of lamps that are currently faulty, with a radius of 170 m and a minimum of three lamps. Each cluster is reported with its centre, radius, number of lamps and a probable cause (a feeder pillar or MCB trip when four or more lamps fail together). **Repeat-failure hotspots** apply DBSCAN to 90 days of complaint locations with a radius of 75 m and at least eight points; the recommended action is to replace the driver or re-cable the stretch rather than repeat spot repairs. Clusters of four or more lamps also raise an alert.'),
  sec('6.2 Lamp Failure-Risk Model'),
  para('A gradient-boosted classifier [6] (140 trees of depth 3, learning rate 0.08, subsampling 0.85) estimates the probability that each lamp fails within 14 days. The features are lamp age, faults in the last 90 days (computed live from real tickets), supply-voltage variation, rainfall in the last seven days, driver type (magnetic ballast, electronic, LED), burning hours and pole condition. Because no real failure records exist, the model is trained on 6,000 synthetic lamp records generated from a logistic relationship plus noise; 4,500 are used for training and 1,500 for testing. The administrator can drag a rainfall slider to see how the risk of all 508 lamps changes. For each lamp the dominant driver is found by replacing each feature in turn with the fleet median and measuring how far the risk falls. Selected lamps can be turned into preventive work orders that join the scheduling queue.'),
  sec('6.3 Weekly Fault Forecast'),
  para('Weekly complaint counts for the last twelve weeks are forecast six weeks ahead with Holt\'s linear method with a damped trend [7], [23]. With level *l*, trend *b*, smoothing constants α and β and damping φ, the recursions are:'),
  eq('l_t = α y_t + (1 − α)(l_{t−1} + φ b_{t−1})'),
  eq('b_t = β (l_t − l_{t−1}) + (1 − β) φ b_{t−1}'),
  eq('ŷ_{t+h} = l_t + (φ + φ² + … + φ^h) b_t'),
  para('The parameters α, β and φ are chosen by grid search to minimise one-step-ahead squared error. The 80% prediction interval uses 1.28 times the standard deviation of past forecast errors, widened with the horizon. The mean absolute percentage error on the demonstration history is about 10%.'),
  sec('6.4 Priority Score'),
  para('Ticket priority combines severity with time and citizen pressure:'),
  eq('priority = severity + min(25, 0.6 × hours open) + min(25, 2.2 × (12 − hours to SLA)) + min(15, 3 × (reports − 1))'),
  para('The third term applies only when fewer than 12 hours remain before the SLA deadline. The formula makes old, nearly-overdue and widely reported faults rise in the queue even when their severity is moderate.'),
  sec('6.5 Technician Scheduling'),
  para('Each on-shift technician has a base location, a set of skills and a shift capacity of 480 minutes. Hazard jobs require an HV-certified electrician; multi-light outages require HV certification and a bucket truck. Travel speed is taken as 24 km/h and the on-site time is the typical repair time of the fault class. The optimiser works as follows:'),
  ...numbered([
    'Sort the open and unstarted tickets by priority, highest first.',
    'For each ticket, try every eligible technician and every position in their route (cheapest insertion), rejecting positions that exceed the shift capacity.',
    'Choose the insertion that increases the route cost least, where route cost = travel kilometres + 2.2 × Σ (priority/100 × hours until the job is reached). The cost rewards short routes and early arrival at urgent jobs.',
    'Improve each route with 2-opt (reverse a segment) [8] and or-opt (move one job) until no move lowers the cost.',
    'Jobs that fit no shift are deferred to the next shift.',
  ], 'num2'),
  para('A **manual baseline** models a busy dispatcher: oldest complaint first, nearest depot, visit in arrival order, skills not checked. The interface shows both plans side by side. A critical ticket bypasses the batch: it is assigned immediately to the nearest eligible technician and placed first in their route.'),
  sec('6.6 Alerts Engine'),
  para('Alerts are recalculated from live data rather than stored: critical faults, tickets past their SLA, tickets within two hours of SLA, high-priority tickets unassigned for three hours, hotspots of four or more lamps, zones with high predicted failure risk, and the size of the review queue. Alerts can be acknowledged and critical ones are pushed to every admin screen as soon as the ticket is created.'),
];

